GBA SLIP networking - host side
===============================

The GBA (GBAED build) runs a small IP host, /usr/sbin/slipd, on its
link-cable UART through /dev/sio.  These two scripts connect it to a
Linux IP interface when the link cable is on a Windows PC and Linux runs
in WSL2 (whose kernel has no SLIP driver):

    GBA --link cable/USB serial (COM3)-- com_bridge.ps1 (Windows)
        --TCP localhost:5555-- slip_tun.py (WSL2) --TUN gba0-- Linux IP

  com_bridge.ps1  copies bytes between the serial port and TCP.  Windows
                  PowerShell 5.1 and .NET only.  RTS/CTS handshaking on.
  slip_tun.py     SLIP-frames IPv4 packets between the TCP connection and
                  a TUN interface.  --fake answers pings itself (no GBA).

One-time setup:

 - WSL2 (as root):  the TUN interface, and a telnet client
       ip tuntap add dev gba0 mode tun user $USER
       ip addr add 192.168.7.1 peer 192.168.7.2 dev gba0
       ip link set gba0 up
       apt install telnet
   gba0 does not survive a WSL restart (wsl --shutdown, a Windows
   reboot); run the three ip commands again then.
 - Windows: copy com_bridge.ps1 to a Windows drive, e.g. C:\Users\Public
   (PowerShell will not run a script from \\wsl.localhost).
 - FTDI cables: Device Manager -> the COM port -> Port Settings ->
   Advanced -> Latency Timer 1 ms (default 16) shortens round trips.

Logging in over telnet:

 1. GBA, on the serial console (Tera Term on COM3, 115200) or the LCD:
    log in as root and start the IP host in the background:
        slipd &
    It takes the UART off the serial console while it runs (the console
    keeps working on the LCD).  `slipd -t 600 &` instead exits after 10
    minutes without traffic, which gives the serial console back.
 2. Disconnect Tera Term (File -> Disconnect), so COM3 is free.
 3. WSL2, terminal 1:    python3 tools/gbanet/slip_tun.py
    Windows (or from WSL2 through powershell.exe), terminal 2:
        powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:\Users\Public\com_bridge.ps1
    It prints "com_bridge: COM3 at 115200 ..."; -Baud must match
    slipd's rate (default 115200 for both, the serial console's too).
 4. WSL2:  ping 192.168.7.2      (RTT about 50-60 ms)
           telnet 192.168.7.2   then log in as root.
    Two sessions can be open at once ("slipd: no free terminal" for a
    third); each runs on its own pty (ttyp0, ttyp1) with TERM=vt100 and
    the client's window size, so vi works.  Expect a few seconds for
    the login prompt and ~0.5 s per command: login, the shell and each
    command are swapped in and out of the GBA's single user window.
    `exit` logs out and closes the connection; if a session stops
    responding, Ctrl-] then `quit` leaves the telnet client.
 5. Other TCP services:  nc 192.168.7.2 7 (echo), 9 (discard),
    19 (chargen).

Stopping:

 1. `exit` every telnet session.
 2. Stop slipd: `kill <pid>` from a telnet session (ps -ax shows it),
    or let `slipd -t N` time out.  The serial console comes back.
 3. Stop slip_tun.py (Ctrl-C); com_bridge.ps1 then exits by itself
    (or Ctrl-C it), freeing COM3 for Tera Term again.
    Packets still in flight when slipd exits reach the console as
    input: expect a line or two of garbage and "syntax error" there,
    and press Enter / Ctrl-C before typing a command.

If the GBA stops answering: hold L+R+SELECT (kernels built with
options HANGDUMP, the default) - it prints the interrupted pc and every
process's state on the LCD without stopping anything; no output means
the kernel is stuck with interrupts off.

Testing slipd without a GBA: `slip_tun.py --pty` runs SLIP on a pty
instead of TCP and prints its name; `make slipd-host` in usr.sbin/slipd
builds slipd for Linux, run as `./slipd-host -d /dev/pts/N`.  --drop P
throws away P% of the packets each way, to exercise TCP's retransmission.

Line rate: the GBA UART has a 4-byte receive FIFO and no "byte arrived"
interrupt, so bytes coming to the GBA are only safe while it polls.
slip_tun.py puts extra END bytes before each packet (--lead, default 40),
so bytes lost while slipd wakes up fall on padding.  Measured with TCP:
115200 gives about 5.4 KB/s to the GBA and 6.5 KB/s from it, with no
retransmits over 100 KB (--lead 12 was too short at this rate); 38400
about 2.3 and 2.8 KB/s.
